@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.Foundations-Spacing a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Foundations: Spacing";
    ds:summary "This document covers both inter-element spacing (gaps between components and patterns) and intra-component spacing (internal padding and border compensation):\n\n- Vertical and horizontal spacing architecture\n- How containers own inter-child spacing across tiers\n- Baseline grid and nudge token architecture\n- Invariants the system must preserve";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "\n\nStatus: Final  \nVersion: 1.1  \nLast updated: 2026-06-29\n\n\n\n## Table of contents\n\n1. [Glossary](#glossary)\n2. [Problem statement and scope](#1-problem-statement-and-scope)\n3. [The vertical spacing architecture](#2-the-vertical-spacing-architecture)\n4. [Horizontal spacing](#3-horizontal-spacing)\n5. [Invariants](#4-invariants)\n\n\n\n## Glossary\n\nCopy of Table 10\n\n\n\n## 1. Problem statement and scope\n\n### 1.1 The problem\n\nOur Design System serves three fundamentally different tiers:\n\n- Application UIs – dashboards, forms, settings pages, admin panels (MAAS, Landscape, Launchpad, Snapcraft, support portal, and ~50 other products)\n- Editorial / marketing sites – [ubuntu.com](http://ubuntu.com), [canonical.com](http://canonical.com), canonical.design, blog content\n- Documentation\n\nEarlier drafts also explored element-owned spacing for some editorial / prose contexts.\n\nFor the current specification, semantic spacing is container-owned throughout the system. Editorial, marketing, documentation, and application UI all follow the same semantic spacing model. Where baseline alignment is enabled, text elements may also carry nudge and baseline-compensation values; those do not replace container-owned semantic spacing.\n\n### 1.2 Scope\n\nThis specification covers both inter-element spacing (gaps between components and patterns) and intra-component spacing (internal padding and border compensation):\n\n- Vertical and horizontal spacing architecture\n- How containers own inter-child spacing across tiers\n- Baseline grid and nudge token architecture\n- Invariants the system must preserve\n\nOut of scope:\n\n- Typography specification (referenced where relevant)\n- Grid system specification (referenced where relevant)\n- Implementation details (CSS architecture, component APIs, tooling)\n\n### 1.3 Goals\n\n1. **Predictability:** Spacing behaviour is deterministic and learnable\n2. Composability: Components and patterns compose correctly without child-specific margin overrides\n3. Single implementation path: The system uses one semantic spacing model across tiers\n4. Tier coverage: Different products can express their needs without changing the semantic spacing model\n\n\n\n## 2. Vertical spacing\n\n### 2.1 Decision\n\nVertical semantic spacing is container-owned everywhere.\n\nThe implementation guidance below applies across application UI, editorial, marketing, and documentation.\n\nCopy of Table 11\n\n### 2.2 Core principle\n\nTable 73\n\nChildren are spacing-neutral externally. Containers define the space between them.\n\n### 2.3 Implementation model\n\nInter-child semantic spacing is owned by containers and patterns, not by child elements.\n\n- Use `gap` or an equivalent container-defined rule for authored semantic spacing.\n- Do not rely on child `margin-top` or semantic `margin-bottom` for authored layout spacing.\n- Reset browser default margins inside design-system scope so native elements stay semantically spacing-neutral.\n\n2.3.1 Ownership rules\n\nContainers and patterns own semantic spacing between direct children.\n\nWhere baseline alignment is enabled, text elements may still carry nudge and baseline compensation.\n\nImplementation guidance\n\n- Use structured containers when prose needs non-uniform semantic transitions.\n- Use nested containers when local density changes, and keep semantic spacing decisions at container boundaries.\n\n2.3.2 Current ownership\n\nInter-child semantic spacing is owned by containers and patterns, not by child elements.\n\n```\nStack       →  gap based on container density\nField group →  gap based on local context\nSection     →  padding / gap owned by container\nButton      →  no external spacing\n```\n\nSpacing decisions stay at container boundaries.\n\nMixed editorial content may still need different semantic transitions between headings, paragraphs, figures, and lists. Where baseline alignment is enabled, the text elements may also carry their own nudge and baseline-compensation values.\n\n\n\nExample: prose wrapper with structured transitions\n\n2.3.3 Structured containers\n\nWhen prose needs non-uniform semantic spacing, that logic belongs to a prose wrapper or pattern rather than to the individual elements.\n\n```\nh2       →  larger gap (owned by prose wrapper)\np        →  standard gap (owned by prose wrapper)\np        →  standard gap (owned by prose wrapper)\nfigure   →  larger gap (owned by prose wrapper)\nh2       →  larger gap (owned by prose wrapper)\n```\n\nThis is how editorial and documentation content are handled in the current specification.\n\nThe ownership model stays the same even when the transitions differ.\n\nCurrent coverage:\n\n- App UI - forms, dashboards, card grids, settings pages\n- Editorial / marketing and documentation content rendered inside prose wrappers\n\n2.3.4 Tier coverage\n\nThe semantic spacing model does not change by product or tier.\n\n- Application UI uses container-owned semantic spacing.\n- Editorial / marketing uses container-owned semantic spacing. Where baseline alignment is enabled, text elements also carry nudge and baseline compensation.\n- Documentation uses container-owned semantic spacing. Where baseline alignment is enabled, text elements also carry nudge and baseline compensation.\n- Rich text / markdown output should be wrapped in a container that owns semantic spacing for its rendered children.\n\nThis keeps one implementation path throughout the system.\n\n2.3.5 Section boundaries\n\nSeparation between sections is owned by the section container, surrounding layout container, or pattern padding.\n\n```css\n.ds-scope :where(h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6, p, ul, ol, dl, figure, blockquote) {\nmargin-block: 0;\n}\n.stack { gap: var(–space-y, 1rem); }\n```\n\nBrowser default margins should be reset in design-system scope so native elements do not reintroduce external child spacing.\n\n2.3.6 Component implementations\n\nComponents are spacing-neutral externally; the container owns inter-child spacing.\n\n**Example: Hero pattern**\n\n```\nHero (padding-bottom: 64px, gap: 24px)\n├── h1 “Sign up today”\n├── p  “Get started free”\n└── [Button: Sign up]\n└── (hero padding-bottom provides the 64px separation to next section)\n\n```\n\nThe section container, not the child elements, owns the spacing boundary to what follows.\n\n### 2.4 Container-owned spacing\n\n2.4.1 Core principle\n\nContainers define spacing between their children. Children stay spacing-neutral externally, and authored layout spacing is expressed through container gaps or equivalent rules.\n\n2.4.2 How it works\n\nA container applies uniform or structured gaps:\n\n```\nContainer with gap (16px)\n├── TextField\n├── TextField\n└── Button\n```\n\nRecommended safeguard: apply a design-system scope margin reset so browser default element margins do not reintroduce external child spacing.\n\n2.4.3 Applying the model across tiers\n\nApplication UI, editorial / marketing, and documentation all use container-owned spacing.\n\nUse the appropriate container type for the content being composed:\n\n- Stack, group, and grid containers for repeated UI\n- Prose wrappers and section containers for mixed editorial content\n- Markdown / rich-text wrappers for rendered documentation content\n- Nested containers where different local densities are needed\n\n**Nested containers for varying density:**\n\nWhen different parts of an interface need different density, nest containers with different gap values. Each container owns the spacing for its direct children.\n\n```\nForm (padding: 16px, gap: 16px)\n├── Field group (gap: 4px)            ← dense: label, input, helper text\n│   ├── Label: \"Upstream DNS\"\n│   ├── Input: \"8.8.8.8\"\n│   └── Helper: \"Comma-separated list of upstream DNS servers.\"\n└── Field group (gap: 4px)            ← same dense group, separated by larger gap\n    ├── Label: \"Default TTL\"\n    ├── Input: \"30\"\n    └── Helper: \"...\"\n```\n\nIn this example, the form’s `gap` separates the two field groups with comfortable spacing. Inside each field group, a small gap keeps the label, input, and helper text tightly bound as a single unit. The form container owns the inter-group spacing; each field group owns its internal density.\n\nThe same ownership rule applies regardless of tier:\n\n- App forms, dashboards, and settings pages\n- Editorial sections, figures, and calls to action\n- Documentation articles, callouts, and examples\n- Markdown or rich-text renderers wrapped by the design system\n\n### 2.4.4 Application-specific container provisions\n\nOne container pattern is unique to application layouts:\n\nFixed-height status bar. A status bar is simply a container with a fixed height. Its width can span both the main area and side drawers, or be scoped to the main area only, depending on use case. Like all application containers, it resets default child margins and owns the spacing between its children.\n\n### 2.5 Intra-component vertical padding\n\nIntra-component vertical padding is component-owned: each component sets its internal top/bottom padding regardless of context.\n\nFor example, buttons, inputs, and cards keep the same internal top/bottom padding whether they appear alone or inside grouped layouts.\n\nWhen borders change the box model, adjust top/bottom padding so outer component height remains aligned to whole-`bU` steps.\n\n### 2.6 Baseline grid and nudges\n\n2.6.1 The baseline grid\n\nIn our design system, the default baseline unit is 4px (previously 8px).  \nWe use the acronym `bU` throughout this specification.\n\n> Box model consideration: When elements have borders, the entire box model must be accounted for in height calculations. For example, an element with padding: 16px and border: 1px should reduce padding to 14px to maintain the same total height. This principle applies across the system.\n\n2.6.2 The nudge technique\n\nThe underlying issue is browser line-height centering: CSS centres text vertically within the line-height box rather than anchoring it to a baseline. This means text baselines can land at inconsistent positions unless they are compensated deliberately.\n\nNudge tokens address that by adding a small `padding-top` to text-bearing elements or components.\n\nIn baseline-aligned contexts, baseline correction is an explicit exception to the semantic spacing rule.\n\nImplementation notes:\n\n- `padding-top = nudge`\n- `margin-bottom = baseline-compensation`\n- Container `gap` or structured transition rule = semantic spacing\n\nSome implementations approximate measured nudges with a cap-height alignment shortcut.\n\n```css\nThat avoids reading and maintaining calculated nudges, but it is less accurate because cap height is only a proxy for the ascender/descender metrics that determine line-box centering.\nThe ownership model does not change: if text is shifted with `padding-top`, the resulting offset still needs compensation.\n.stack {\ngap: var(–space-y, 1rem);\n}\n.baseline-text {\npadding-top: var(–nudge, 0px);\nmargin-bottom: var(–baseline-comp, 0px);\n}\n```\n\nThis preserves the architecture: containers still own semantic spacing, while text elements own the correction needed to land their baselines on the grid.\n\n2.6.3 Scope\n\nBaseline alignment is currently enabled for editorial / marketing and documentation. In those contexts, text elements own nudge and baseline compensation while containers still own semantic spacing. Application UI continues to use the same container-owned semantic spacing model without non-zero nudge compensation.\n\nCopy of Table 13\n\n\n\n## 3. Horizontal spacing\n\nHorizontal spacing uses a container-owned model throughout the system.\n\nUnlike vertical spacing, horizontal spacing rarely encodes typographic hierarchy between adjacent elements. Large separations are typically set by the grid (gutters, margins, keylines). What remains is spacing between grouped elements inside components and patterns, where container-defined gaps are simpler and more consistent.\n\n### 3.1 Layout-level spacing\n\nHorizontal spacing at the layout level is handled by the grid system:\n\nCopy of Table 14\n\nThe grid provides consistent horizontal rhythm across breakpoints and establishes keylines: shared horizontal offsets that align text and controls across components and patterns, improving scannability. Components placed within grid columns inherit this spacing automatically.\n\n> Two-dimensional grids: When layout produces a two-dimensional grid of items (card grids, dashboards, image galleries), the vertical gap between rows must equal the horizontal gutter (24px). This keeps items on a uniform spatial grid in both axes.\n\n### 3.2 Horizontal sibling spacing\n\nHorizontal spacing between sibling elements is container-owned: the parent context sets the gap.\n\nFor example, in a row of buttons or chips, the container defines the horizontal gap between items.\n\n\n\n### 3.3 Intra-component horizontal padding\n\nIntra-component horizontal padding is component-owned: each component sets its internal left and right padding regardless of context.\n\nFor example, buttons, inputs, and chips keep the same internal left and right padding whether they appear alone or inside grouped layouts.\n\nWhen borders change the box model, adjust left and right padding to preserve content box width. For bottom-only borders, left and right padding stays unchanged.\n\nCopy of Table 15\n\n\n\n## 4. Invariants\n\nInvariants are the non-negotiable rules this spacing model must preserve. They serve as evaluation criteria for architectural decisions and acceptance criteria for contributions.\n\n### 4.1 The invariant list\n\nCopy of Table 16\n\n### 4.2 How the invariants apply\n\nSubstitutability: containers own semantic spacing contracts, so children can be exchanged without inherited semantic-spacing side effects.\n\n- Locality: components own internal padding and any baseline correction; containers own inter-child semantic spacing.\n- No double spacing: semantic spacing does not stack with parent spacing. Where baseline compensation uses `margin-bottom`, it must remain distinct from the semantic spacing term.\n- Composition isolation: semantic spacing rules stay at container boundaries.\n\nPredictable removal: removing a child leaves the remaining container spacing intact.\n\n- Grid alignment: components and patterns should resolve to whole-bU outer heights.\n- Where baseline correction is required, the text element owns nudge and baseline compensation.\n\n### 4.3 Invariant priority\n\nWhen invariants conflict, resolve in this order:\n\n1. **Substitutability** – composition must remain predictable\n2. **Locality** – knowledge shouldn’t leak across boundaries\n3. **No double spacing** – additive bugs break layouts\n4. **Composition isolation** – parent-child contracts must be stable\n5. **Grid alignment** – vertical rhythm should be maintained\n6. **Predictable removal** – overrides must be safe\n\n> Grid alignment refers to outer component height in whole-bU steps; baseline correction is handled by nudges where needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n## 5. Authoring guidance\n\nThis section translates the architectural model into concrete decisions for the audiences who apply it daily.\n\n### 5.1 Component designers\n\nComponents are spacing-neutral externally, except for baseline compensation where baseline alignment is enabled.\n\nIf tiers use different nudge values, that is a token decision rather than a different semantic spacing architecture.\n\nCore rules\n\nComponents do not own inter-child semantic spacing.\n\n- Internal vertical padding is component-owned; use values from the spacing scale.\n- If the component contains text and uses nudge tokens, preserve outer height through the defined baseline-compensation term or equivalent internal box-model compensation.\n- If the component has a border that changes the box model, reduce padding to keep total height on whole-`bU` steps.\n\nAdditional rules\n\nInternal horizontal padding is always component-owned.\n\n- Do not ship authored layout dependence on semantic `margin-top` or `margin-bottom`.\n- Keep internal padding consistent regardless of layout context.\n- Text-bearing components apply nudge tokens where required.\n\nPre-review checklist\n\n- Spacing values use tokens, not arbitrary px\n- No authored layout dependence on child `margin-top` or semantic `margin-bottom`\n- Component height is whole-bU multiples (account for borders)\n\nVerification\n\n- Text-bearing components apply nudge tokens where required\n- Tested in composition inside a stack, a grid cell, and a prose wrapper where relevant\n- Container-owned spacing is used consistently across apps, editorial, and documentation\n- Where prose needs varied transitions, use a structured container rather than child-owned external spacing\n- Use tokenized gaps and padding values rather than ad hoc spacing fixes\n\n### 5.2 Pattern designers\n\nPatterns compose components. The principle: patterns own spacing between their children.\n\nUse uniform stack containers for repeated UI.\n\nUse structured prose wrappers for mixed editorial content when different transitions need different values.\n\n- Use nested containers where different local densities are needed.\n- Do not add one-off spacing overrides to individual children when the spacing decision belongs to the container.\n- For two-dimensional grids, set the vertical row gap equal to the horizontal gutter (24px).\n- Wrap markdown or rich-text output in a container that owns spacing for rendered children.\n\nPattern variants\n\nIf a pattern needs tighter or looser spacing, change the container recipe or use a component variant that keeps external spacing neutral.\n\n- Keep spacing decisions at the container or pattern boundary.\n- Do not depend on child `margin-bottom` to solve composition problems.\n\nSlot-based patterns\n\nWhen a pattern passes arbitrary children through a slot, wrap the slot in a container that owns spacing for that child list.\n\n- Do not depend on slotted children to provide their own external margins.\n- Document expected child types where the slot is constrained.\n- Use the same ownership model in apps, editorial, and documentation.\n\n\n\n\n\n## Appendices\n\n### A. References\n\n- Material Design 3: [Layout spacing](https://m3.material.io/foundations/layout/understanding-layout/spacing#9ebf5386-23ca-400a-b68d-e8c679a4a19d)\n- Shopify Polaris: [Stack](https://shopify.dev/docs/api/checkout-ui-extensions/latest/polaris-web-components/layout-and-structure/stack)\n  - Atlassian Design System: [Stack](https://atlassian.design/components/primitives/stack/)\n\n";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.Explanation.
